#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Show Google Calendar on a jailbroken Kindle Voyage as an always-on wall calendar.

Fetches each calendar's secret iCal feed (Google Calendar → Settings → the
calendar → "Secret address in iCal format"), expands recurring events over the
next 7 days, lays the agenda out as HTML — today in detail, the coming days
compact, the month grid in the header — screenshots it with headless Chrome or
Chromium at the Voyage's 1072x1448 (300 ppi), and pushes the PNG to the Kindle
over SSH, where FBInk from the USBNetwork package paints it. The push also
turns the Kindle into a kiosk: the reader framework is stopped so nothing
repaints over the calendar, powerd is told not to sleep, and a keeper loop is
left running that re-enables Wi-Fi whenever it drops — the power button still
suspends the device, and after the wake nothing else would reconnect it.
`--restore` undoes all of it; a reboot brings the reader back on its own and
the next push re-applies the kiosk.

Meant to run as a container (Dockerfile and compose.yaml alongside) that
repaints every 10 minutes with `--every 600`; a bare `python3 voyage-calendar`
works anywhere with Chrome or Chromium and an ssh client.

Configuration comes from the environment (a feed URL is a credential: anyone
holding it reads the calendar):
  VOYAGE_CALENDAR_ICS_URLS   whitespace-separated feed URLs, or local .ics paths
                             (quotes around the whole list are fine)
  VOYAGE_CALENDAR_EMAIL      optional: your address, to drop invitations you
                             declined
  VOYAGE_CALENDAR_HOST       the Kindle: an ssh host alias, an address, or
                             user@address (default: voyage; the user defaults
                             to root, as on every jailbroken Kindle)
  VOYAGE_CALENDAR_SSH_KEY    optional: private key for the Kindle. With it set,
                             ssh offers only that key and keeps the Kindle's
                             host key under the cache directory, so no ssh
                             config or known_hosts is needed (a container)
  VOYAGE_CALENDAR_CHROME     optional: the Chrome or Chromium binary (default:
                             Google Chrome on macOS, else chromium or
                             google-chrome on PATH)
  VOYAGE_CALENDAR_CACHE_DIR  optional: where the last good copy of each feed
                             lives (default: ~/.cache/voyage-calendar)
  LOG_TIMESTAMP              optional: set it to prefix every log line with
                             the time

A feed that fails to download is served from its last good copy and the
footer says so.

Remote commands target the Kindle's BusyBox environment: scripts appear only
as `sh` in `ps`, so inspect `/proc/*/cmdline`; `timeout` takes `-t N`; `/sbin`
is absent from the SSH PATH; and device state is managed through the powerd,
wifid, and cmd properties exposed by `lipc-get-prop` and `lipc-set-prop`.

Recurrence covers what Google Calendar writes: DAILY, WEEKLY (BYDAY, WKST),
MONTHLY (BYMONTHDAY, ordinal BYDAY such as 2TU or -1FR), YEARLY, with
INTERVAL, COUNT, UNTIL, EXDATE, RDATE, and per-instance overrides
(RECURRENCE-ID, including moved and cancelled instances).

Modes:
  (default)     fetch, render, push to the Kindle, and paint
  --every SECS  repeat the default mode forever, SECS apart; a failed round
                is logged and the next one tried
  -n, --dry-run fetch and render; print the agenda as text instead of pushing
  --landscape   two columns for a Kindle on its side (top edge to the left):
                weekday, month and today on the left, the week on the right
  --png PATH    also save the rendered screenshot to PATH
  --html PATH   also save the rendered page to PATH (to iterate on the layout)
  --restore     hand the Kindle back to the reader UI (start the framework,
                allow sleep) and exit
  --test        run unit tests

Exit codes: 0 ok · 1 fetch, render, or push failed · 3 no feed configured
"""
